WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.928
For sixteen values and eight threads per

00:00:02.928 --> 00:00:05.356
block, each thread in block zero copies

00:00:05.356 --> 00:00:07.784
one value into scratch. Scratch is shared

00:00:07.784 --> 00:00:10.558
memory that the threads in the same block

00:00:10.558 --> 00:00:11.252
can access.

00:00:12.752 --> 00:00:15.286
Thread one stores two, while thread five

00:00:15.286 --> 00:00:17.820
stores six. Each must reach the block

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:19.991
barrier before any thread continues. The

00:00:19.991 --> 00:00:22.525
barrier lets thread one read the value

00:00:22.525 --> 00:00:23.973
written by thread five.

00:00:25.473 --> 00:00:28.121
At a distance of four, thread one reads

00:00:28.121 --> 00:00:31.101
scratch at index five and adds six to its

00:00:31.101 --> 00:00:33.087
two. The notebook experiment prints those

00:00:33.087 --> 00:00:35.073
two inputs from inside the kernel.

00:00:36.573 --> 00:00:39.318
In the nineteen value case, the last block

00:00:39.318 --> 00:00:41.721
starts with three inputs and five zeros.

00:00:41.721 --> 00:00:43.780
All eight threads reach the barrier.

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:46.525
Compare the sum of those three inputs with

00:00:46.525 --> 00:00:48.584
the last output printed by main.
